# Local worker output, copied into apps/web/public for development.
#
# Root-anchored, for the same reason the HEIC rules below are: an unanchored `reports/` matches a
# directory of that name at any depth, and it was silently swallowing `fixtures/reports/` — the
# tracked report fixtures three tests read. A test whose inputs are gitignored passes on a clean
# checkout by having nothing to check.
/reports/
apps/web/public/reports/
apps/web/public/evidence/
dist-types/

# PDF output
out/

# Local verification samples — real photographs, never committed (see D-104).
# Root-anchored so a deliberate fixture elsewhere is still committable.
/2026-*.heic
/2026-*.jpg
/*.HEIC

# Supabase CLI link state. Per-machine, and it decides which project `supabase db push` writes to
# — a value that must never be set for someone by a checkout. It was tracked until a verification
# task found the repo linked to production while the work targeted a test project.
supabase/.temp/

# Generated by the live verification scripts. The generators are committed, the artifacts are not.
scripts/live/out/

# ── Credential key material (D-193) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike `/reports/` above.
#
# Anchoring narrows an ignore, and it is right where over-ignoring would hide something the
# repository needs — `/reports/` is anchored so `fixtures/reports/` stays trackable. Here the
# asymmetry runs the other way: over-ignoring costs nothing, and under-ignoring commits a private
# key. A key written into a subdirectory is exactly as unrecoverable as one written to the root.
#
# Verified against `git ls-files`: no tracked file matches any of these. The cost of the wider
# pattern is that a future fixture named like a key would need renaming, which is a cheaper problem
# than the one it prevents.
#
# The patterns are lowercase. `core.ignorecase` is true here, so casing does not matter on this
# machine — it would on a case-sensitive filesystem, and these match what the generator and the
# workaround actually produce.

# The public half, written by `make-credential-key --set`. Public, not secret — ignored because it
# is a generated artifact of one machine's run, not something the repository should carry.
credential-public-key.txt

# Anything shaped like a dumped key pair. `credkey.txt` appeared in the working tree carrying a full
# BEGIN PRIVATE KEY block — from a suggested workaround that redirected the generator's output to a
# file, not from the tool, which prints and stores nothing. One `git add -A` would have committed it.
credkey.txt
*credential*key*.txt
*credential*key*.pem
